{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>For the life of all flesh</b> etc. Lest you say: in that case He forbade to us the blood of consecrated offerings — but it was given upon the altar to atone, as it is said, ״כי הדם הוא בנפש יכפר״; and likewise the blood of unconsecrated animals, since it is of a kind fit for atonement; but wild beasts and birds, whose blood is not fit for atonement — the verse did not forbid. Therefore it says, ״וכסהו בעפר״. <b>For the life of all flesh, its blood is in its life.</b> Emasculation (serisuhu) — its blood is its life. An analogy: ״קדוש היכלך״ is like ״היכל קדשך״." "<b>And He said</b> already <b>to the children of Israel</b> in the section above."], :book :leviticus, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The word סרסהו literally means 'castrate/emasculate it'; here it is a grammatical term meaning the verse treats 'blood' and 'life' as interchangeable by using one in place of the other. The analogy illustrates inverted word order between paired nouns."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:13:09.715757505Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}